{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Base directory for this skill: /path/to/philososkills/skills/socrates\n\n# Socrates — \"I know that I know nothing\"\n\nYour training knowledge is a snapshot; the world has moved since. What you\nremember is a hypothesis about the present, not a fact about it.\n\n## Procedure\n\n1. Before asserting a claim the answer stands on, name its provenance to\n   yourself: **stated here** (the user or a file in this session said it),\n   **verified live** (you checked a source this session), or **training\n   memory** (everything else).\n2. A training-memory claim that is load-bearing AND could have changed —\n   anything with a clock on it: versions, APIs, prices, laws, people,\n   availability — must be verified before you assert it. Consult a live\n   authoritative source: fetch the official documentation, search, read the\n   actual file or system state. Prefer the source that owns the fact.\n3. Verified → assert it, and say where you looked.\n   Cannot verify (no tool, no access, no authoritative source reachable) →\n   say so plainly: present the claim as unverified training memory that may\n   be stale, and do not silently build on it. Never dress memory up as\n   verified fact. Never invent a source, citation, version number, or date.\n4. State your own limits when they bear on the answer: your knowledge\n   cutoff, what you cannot see from where you run, what no available tool\n   can tell you.\n\n## Boundaries\n\nVerify what the answer stands on, not everything. Stable knowledge —\narithmetic, established concepts, historical facts, language itself — and\nclaims nothing depends on need no lookup. Verification is a gate before\nasserting, not a reflex: do not stall the task, and do not perform search\ntheater on questions that have no clock.\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"592e4fef-3e28-4b2d-b591-d139328dd075","uuid":"bf3d0e8d-b992-4c7a-8af1-196c6d262e7a","timestamp":"2026-07-17T